tact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Varaha Lakshmi Narasimha temple, Simhachalam), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":"Kalinga","construction_century":13},{"id":"7aee8c14-1065-486c-901c-11ce095b13c5","name":"Ambalapuzha Sree Krishna Temple","deity":"Krishna","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Kerala","district":"Alappuzha","town":"Ambalapuzha","latitude":9.3833,"longitude":76.35,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"pure_veg_available","food_source_name":"Palpayasam prasadam counter — the temple's famous sweet milk payasam, distributed after the midday pooja","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":0,"nearest_river_name":"mammudu thodu River","river_distance_km":3.7,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb; the temple's own festival and the daily Palpayasam offering draw visitors year-round.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-04T13:57:28.972216+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Built by the Chembakasserry ruler Devanarayana Thampuran between the 15th and 17th centuries, who is said to have dedicated his entire kingdom to Krishna and ruled afterward only as the deity's regent. The temple is famous for Palpayasam, a milk-and-rice offering said to have originated when a sage — revealed to be Krishna himself — beat the king at chess using the classic doubling-grains-of-rice wager, and asked that payasam be offered daily until the debt was paid; it still is, centuries later. During Tipu Sultan's raids in 1789, the deity from Guruvayur was kept here for safekeeping for three years.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"അമ്പലപ്പുഴ ശ്രീകൃഷ്ണക്ഷേത്രം","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_na