nificance":[],"country":"India","state":"Odisha","district":"Bhadrak","town":"Tihidi","latitude":21.0275,"longitude":86.61111,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Salandi River","river_distance_km":6.8,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:03:52.882408+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A sun temple at Palia village near Bhadrak in Odisha, evidence of Surya worship in the region. The sanctum holds a tapering stone slab with four relief figures of the Sun on its sides, each carrying lotuses and standing on a seven-horse chariot. The structure dates to the 13th century and was rebuilt in the early 20th century.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Biju Patnaik International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":119.7,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Bhadrak Junction railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":12.1,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Biranchinarayan Temple, Palia), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":13},{"id":"7f770e6a-4859-4399-8511-5b87cb07b00c","name":"Kaleshwara Mukteshwara Swamy Temple","deity":"Kaleshwara (Shiva) / Mukteshwara (Yama-linga)","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":["Trilinga Kshetra"],"country":"India","state":"Telangana","district":"Bhupalpally","town":"Kaleshwaram","latitude":18.81194,"longitude":79.90389,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Godavari–Pranahita confluence","river_distance_km":0.2,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-05T11:47:46.109676+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:35.665826+00:00","sthala_purana":"One of the three Trilinga Kshetras traditionally said to mark the ancient boundaries of the Telugu-speaking land — along with Srisailam and Draksharamam in Andhra Pradesh — Kaleshwaram stands at the meeting point of the Godavari, Pranahita, and a third stream locally identified with the Saraswati. Its sanctum is unusual for holding two lingas on a single pedestal, worshipped together as Kaleshwara (Shiva) and Mukteshwara, the latter associated with Yama, the god of death — pilgrims traditionally visit believing worship here can free one from Yama's hold. The shrine has four doors, one on each side, allowing entry from any direction. First built under the Chalukyas, with major additions under the Kakatiya dynasty.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"శ్రీ కాళేశ్వర ముక్తీశ్వర స్వామి దేవాలయం","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Ramagundam Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":55.2,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Ravindrakhani railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":48.5,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition (Trilinga Desham)","architecture_style":"Dravidian/Deccan (Chalukya origins, Kakatiya expansion)","construction_century":null},{"id":"7f8f5888-f147-4f9b-b5da-7e4d6ba5a244","name":"Neer Vanna Perumal Temple, Thiruneermalai","deity":"Neervannan (Vishnu)","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Chengalpattu","town":"Thiruneermalai","latitude":12.9503,"longitude":80.1342,"sandhya_